"In the early 1600s, the Dutch East India Company, a trading company from the Netherlands, wanted to trade with China. Ming officials would not let it set up a base on China’s coast, or on the Penghu Islands. In September 1624, at their request, the Company left Penghu and moved to Tayouan, a sandbar near today’s Anping in Tainan. The understanding was that Chinese merchants would be allowed to come and trade there.": "9358f94e6246",
"In the early 1600s, the Dutch East India Company, a trading company from the Netherlands, wanted to trade with China. Ming officials would not let it set up a base on China’s coast, or on the Penghu Islands. In September 1624, at their request, the Company left Penghu and moved to Tayouan, a sandbar near today’s Anping in Tainan. The understanding was that Chinese merchants would be allowed to come and trade there. At Tayouan the Company built a fort. Work began in 1624, the fort was named Zeelandia in 1627, and it was finished in 1634. A print showing Tayouan in 1632 has it on a hill by the water, with pointed corners, wooden fences on the slope, and ships in the bay. It became the Company’s military, trading, and government center in Taiwan. Tayouan was not empty land. Siraya villages lived nearby, among them Sinkan, Soulang, Bakloan, and Mattau. In 1627 Sinkan built a house for Company soldiers, but in 1629, 63 Company soldiers were killed at Mattau. In 1635 the Company attacked Mattau, with Sinkan warriors at its side, and burned the village. Mattau then signed a treaty, written by the Company, giving up its land. A trading post was turning into a colony. The Company’s main business was buying Chinese silk at Tayouan and selling it in Japan. From 1635 to 1639, raw silk and silk cloth made up 87 percent of everything the Company’s trading posts sent to Japan. Taiwan’s own best-known product was deerskins: Japanese traders said they had come to buy them six years before the Dutch, and in 1636 Taiwan exported 60,440 skins. Indigenous villagers hunted deer; from 1636 to 1645, the Company also licensed Chinese hunters. From the 1630s, Chinese farmers came to grow sugarcane and rice. The Company bought their sugar at a guaranteed price and sold it as far away as Japan and Persia, and every Chinese farmer paid a head tax. From 1644, Chinese traders bid for the right to be the only trader in a village. In 1652 Chinese settlers rose up against the Company, in what is called the Guo Huaiyi incident. Afterward, the Company built another fort, Provintia, at Chikan. Did Taiwan make money for the Company? Historians disagree. Some read the account books as showing large profits; the economist Wu Tsong-min recalculated them and found a profit in only a few years. In 1662 the Company surrendered to Zheng Chenggong and left. That December, its council in Batavia had doubts about sending a force to retake Taiwan, and suggested looking for a trading harbor farther north instead.": "39389c1f0c5d",
